---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import UnitConverter from '@/components/tools/units/UnitConverter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="volume-converter">
  <UnitConverter client:load dimension="volume" from="l" to="gal-us" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert a volume</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Type into either field. The volume converter works in both directions, so litres to gallons and gallons to litres use the same control.</li>
      <li>Choose the units carefully where a US and an imperial version both exist. The list puts them in separate groups and labels each symbol, for example "gal (US)" and "gal (UK)".</li>
      <li>Use the chart below for a range of values, for example to scale a tank, a batch or a recipe.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>US and imperial units are not the same</h2>
    <p>
      This causes more wrong volume conversions than anything else. The US and UK use the same names for
      different sizes:
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Unit</th><th>US</th><th>Imperial (UK)</th><th>Difference</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Gallon</td><td>3.785 L</td><td>4.546 L</td><td>UK is 20 % larger</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Quart</td><td>0.946 L</td><td>1.137 L</td><td>UK is 20 % larger</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pint</td><td>473 mL</td><td>568 mL</td><td>UK is 20 % larger</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fluid ounce</td><td>29.57 mL</td><td>28.41 mL</td><td>US is 4 % larger</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The fluid ounce is the other way round because a US pint is 16 fl oz and an imperial pint is 20. So a
      British pint of beer is about 95 mL bigger than an American one, although a British fluid ounce is the
      smaller unit.
    </p>

    <h2>Which factors are exact</h2>
    <p>
      The imperial gallon is defined as exactly 4.54609 litres. The US liquid gallon is defined as exactly 231
      cubic inches, and the inch is exactly 25.4 mm, so it is exactly 3.785411784 litres. The other units in
      each system divide from its gallon without rounding: quarts are quarters, pints are eighths, and US
      fluid ounces are 128ths.
    </p>
    <p>
      The litre is exactly one cubic decimetre, so 1 mL and 1 cm³ are the same volume, and engine displacement
      can be given in cc or litres. The oil barrel is exactly 42 US gallons, a figure from the 19th-century
      Pennsylvania oil trade, now fixed by convention and not by any physical barrel.
    </p>

    <h2>Everyday container volumes</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Container</th><th>Metric</th><th>US</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Espresso shot</td><td>30 mL</td><td>1 fl oz</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Soft drink can</td><td>330 mL / 355 mL</td><td>11.2 / 12 fl oz</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wine bottle</td><td>750 mL</td><td>25.4 fl oz</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Milk carton</td><td>1 L</td><td>33.8 fl oz</td></tr>
          <tr><td>US milk jug</td><td>3.785 L</td><td>1 gallon</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Small car fuel tank</td><td>45 L</td><td>11.9 US gal</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bathtub</td><td>150 L</td><td>39.6 US gal</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>When you need weight instead of volume</h2>
    <p>
      Volume and weight are interchangeable only for water, where one litre weighs one kilogram by design. For
      other substances the answer depends on density: a litre of vegetable oil is about 918 g, a litre of honey
      about 1.44 kg. To convert a recipe from cups to grams, use the cooking converter, which has a density
      table for common baking ingredients.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
